From 165f28e869596b47ae1118752161fd7acd35d3fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: J P <jpwatters@mac.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 27/36] TCI for WS / WSJT-X / JTDX on the Mac; relay serves a
 listen-only I/Q copy on 7357

WS and WSJT-X on macOS could not open the bridge's pseudo-terminal serial
port: its log shows they never sent it a byte. The bridge now runs a TCI
server (ws://127.0.0.1:40001, `--tci`, the installer's default), which TCI
programs connect to as a network radio:

- receive: the relay sends a copy of the 48 kHz I/Q on its own port (7357,
  listen only, one client), and the bridge makes USB audio from it (dial to
  dial+3.2 kHz, slow AGC) and streams it over TCI;
- control: TCI vfo/modulation/trx become FA/MD/TX;/RX; on QMX serial port 3;
  the radio's frequency and mode are read back every second; Q9 1; is set if
  the radio is not in I/Q mode;
- transmit: the program's FT8/FT4/JS8/WSPR/tune audio is requested in real time
  (TX chrono, 10 ms), each tone is measured from its zero crossings once it has
  settled, and sent to the QMX as TA<Hz>; on a fixed 50 ms delay from the
  half-way point of the transition, so symbols keep their spacing. A 3 s
  no-audio watchdog keys the radio up; a disconnect mid-transmission sends
  TA0;RX;.

The Tab5's own connection (7355) is unchanged and runs alongside. install-mac.sh
installs numpy for macOS's Python and sets up TCI mode (MODE=serial for the old
serial port); --probe also checks the I/Q copy.

Bench test against the relay's fake radio (tools/mac-serial-bridge/test/
tci_client_test.py): receive audio peak at 1500 Hz for a signal 1500 Hz above
the dial; 12 GFSK FT8 tones sent at 160 ms +-1 ms spacing, within 0.2 Hz;
nettest (Tab5 I/Q 48,160 pairs/s, CAT, menus, port 3) passed at the same time.

